# Byte-fidelity oracle fixtures under tests/testdata are pinned to their native
# line endings so a fresh checkout is byte-identical on Windows, Linux and macOS.
# git still normalizes the stored blobs to LF on commit — only the working-tree
# EOL is pinned here, so the committed oracle files are never rewritten.
#
# .vis project files and .def/.ifb catalog files: the SDK serializers reproduce
# the vendor's exact bytes, including native CRLF line endings (each writer
# hardcodes "\r\n"), and the byte-exact round-trip/authoring tests compare with
# SequenceEqual — so these must check out as CRLF on every OS.
*.vis   text eol=crlf
*.def   text eol=crlf
*.ifb   text eol=crlf

# Findings-export oracles: real ExportFindings output, so the working tree must
# hold the writer's exact bytes. Same treatment as the .vis oracles above and for
# the same reason — the writer emits the .vis encoding (ISO-8859-1, no BOM) with
# hardcoded CRLF, and the oracle test compares the bytes. Like those files these
# are not valid UTF-8, so a UTF-8 diff viewer renders the Danish messages as
# mojibake; that is the established trade for a byte-exact Latin-1 oracle and is
# not worked around here. Do NOT add working-tree-encoding: it would make the
# working-tree bytes depend on the git client, and a client that ignores it
# writes the wrong bytes.
tests/testdata/validation/*.xml   text eol=crlf

# Report oracles: the SDK report generator emits LF, and the oracle tests strip
# CR before comparing. Pin them to LF everywhere for a deterministic, churn-free
# checkout on all OSes (no autocrlf CRLF conversion on Windows).
tests/testdata/reports/*.html   text eol=lf
tests/testdata/reports/*.txt    text eol=lf
